WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.419
Another new feature in Unreal Engine 5 is mass entity.

2
00:00:06.419 --> 00:00:09.580
This is a framework for data oriented calculations.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.259
A mass entity's primary data structure is the fragment.

4
00:00:13.259 --> 00:00:15.179
A fragment is a data only element

5
00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:16.899
and it represents an atomic piece of data

6
00:00:16.899 --> 00:00:19.420
that can be used in calculations.

7
00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:20.859
Examples of fragments are things

8
00:00:20.859 --> 00:00:23.379
such as transforms and velocity.

9
00:00:23.379 --> 00:00:25.859
It's important to note that fragments cannot be created

10
00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:27.690
in blueprint at present.

11
00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:29.690
Fragments can be grouped into collections

12
00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.329
and these collections can then be associated

13
00:00:31.329 --> 00:00:32.689
with an ID.

14
00:00:32.689 --> 00:00:36.460
This collection of fragments is then what we call an entity.

15
00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.060
The idea associated with the entity

16
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.060
can be used for quick lookups

17
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:42.659
and creating an entity is similar to class instance

18
00:00:42.659 --> 00:00:44.880
in object orientated programming.

19
00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:47.200
However, instead of directly declaring a class

20
00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:48.420
and its functionality,

21
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:52.500
entities are built up by fragment composition.

22
00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:53.659
An example of an entity,

23
00:00:53.659 --> 00:00:56.939
we can take two fragments such as transform and velocity.

24
00:00:56.939 --> 00:00:58.780
These two fragments now form our entity

25
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:01.950
which would then give a unique ID.

26
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:03.109
Again, it's important to note

27
00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:05.349
that entities are data only elements

28
00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:09.450
so they have no internal logic within them.

29
00:01:09.450 --> 00:01:12.489
Moving one step further, we have archetypes.

30
00:01:12.489 --> 00:01:14.430
Archetypes are a collection of entities

31
00:01:14.430 --> 00:01:16.930
of an identical composition.

32
00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.650
Archetypes are composed of different types of fragments

33
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.689
in a specific configuration.

34
00:01:21.689 --> 00:01:23.329
Taking our previous example of an entity

35
00:01:23.329 --> 00:01:25.409
made up of a transform and a velocity,

36
00:01:25.409 --> 00:01:26.890
we can build a collection of entities

37
00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:28.670
that hold the same configuration,

38
00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:31.030
giving us our archetype.

39
00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:33.049
So let's take a look at a visualization

40
00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:34.569
of an archetype.

41
00:01:34.569 --> 00:01:36.129
We can see that we have an archetype

42
00:01:36.129 --> 00:01:38.189
that holds an entity made up of transforms

43
00:01:38.189 --> 00:01:40.480
and velocities as per our example.

44
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:41.879
Now let's have a look at how that archetype

45
00:01:41.879 --> 00:01:44.200
is organized internally.

46
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.959
Entities of an archetype are organized in memory chunks.

47
00:01:47.959 --> 00:01:49.519
Arranging data in chunks like this

48
00:01:49.519 --> 00:01:51.620
ensures best performance when retrieving fragments

49
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.079
associated with entities

50
00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:55.909
of the same archetype from memory.

51
00:01:55.909 --> 00:01:57.590
Processes are stateless classes

52
00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:00.629
that supply the processing logic for fragments.

53
00:02:00.629 --> 00:02:02.150
It makes use of entity queries

54
00:02:02.189 --> 00:02:03.909
to specify which types of fragments

55
00:02:03.909 --> 00:02:07.140
they need to perform their operations on.

56
00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.620
A good example of a processor

57
00:02:08.620 --> 00:02:10.699
is the mass apply movement processor,

58
00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:13.020
which is already present in the latest version

59
00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:14.580
of Unreal Engine 5.

60
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:17.039
Entity queries provide batches of fragments

61
00:02:17.039 --> 00:02:18.419
to the processes with their regard

62
00:02:18.419 --> 00:02:21.770
to individual entity identifiers.

63
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:22.789
As we can see here,

64
00:02:22.789 --> 00:02:25.229
we have an entity query that will return our processor

65
00:02:25.229 --> 00:02:27.849
to arrays of transforms and velocities,

66
00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:31.039
the exact makeup of our example entity.

67
00:02:31.039 --> 00:02:34.919
A tag is a trivial fragment that contains no data.

68
00:02:34.919 --> 00:02:37.000
The existence or absence of a tag

69
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.680
is used as data in its own right.

70
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.560
Tags are part of the entity's composition.

71
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.120
A chunk fragment is a fragment

72
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.300
associated with a chunk instead of an entity.

73
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.860
Chunk fragments are used to store per chunk data,

74
00:02:51.860 --> 00:02:54.159
which are used in managerial processing

75
00:02:54.159 --> 00:02:57.340
like calculating levels of detail.

76
00:02:57.340 --> 00:02:59.740
They're also used as part of the entity composition

77
00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:01.900
system much like tags.

78
00:03:02.419 --> 00:03:03.460
Moving on to the last major addition

79
00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.900
to Unreal Engine 5's AI suite of tools,

80
00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.419
we have the zone graph.

81
00:03:07.419 --> 00:03:08.719
The zone graph was envisioned

82
00:03:08.719 --> 00:03:10.500
to be a lightweight navigation system

83
00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:11.699
that allowed for pathfinding

84
00:03:11.699 --> 00:03:14.020
even with non-stream portions of the world,

85
00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:18.159
and for general control of direction and navigation.

86
00:03:18.159 --> 00:03:19.680
The zone graph uses metadata

87
00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.800
to represent specific navigation flows

88
00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.250
such as traffic lanes, sidewalks, or wood trails.

89
00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.710
This metadata is represented

90
00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.750
as a point-to-point corridor structure

91
00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:31.870
connected by segments.

92
00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:32.710
In the next few slides,

93
00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:34.370
we're gonna take a look how we can define

94
00:03:34.370 --> 00:03:36.389
our own corridor structures.

95
00:03:36.389 --> 00:03:37.509
There's a few critical classes

96
00:03:37.509 --> 00:03:41.599
we need to take a look at when defining a zone graph.

97
00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:43.800
Firstly, we have the zone shape.

98
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:46.099
Zone shapes define the area and shape

99
00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:48.199
of the zone graph lanes.

100
00:03:48.199 --> 00:03:49.539
Zone shapes need to be configured

101
00:03:49.539 --> 00:03:51.280
with a lane profile which determines

102
00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:53.340
the number of lanes, tags, direction,

103
00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.780
and width of the lane.

104
00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:58.439
Next, we have the zone graph data.

105
00:03:58.439 --> 00:04:00.960
The zone graph data is the built navigation data

106
00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:02.340
for the zone graph,

107
00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.659
and it's very similar to the Raycast actor

108
00:04:04.659 --> 00:04:07.590
that is present when using nav mesh.

109
00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.650
Zone graph data is not built automatically

110
00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.310
whilst the user is editing.

111
00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:12.729
It's instead built on demand

112
00:04:12.729 --> 00:04:17.189
via the build zone graph tool from the menu bar.

113
00:04:17.189 --> 00:04:19.629
However, we can enable automatic zone graph building

114
00:04:19.629 --> 00:04:21.470
by enabling the build zone graph

115
00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:25.050
whilst editing flag in the project settings.

116
00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:27.529
Next, we have the zone graph testing actor,

117
00:04:27.529 --> 00:04:29.410
which is used for testing if navigation is possible

118
00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:31.089
between two testing actors,

119
00:04:31.089 --> 00:04:33.569
similar to the traditional navigation testing actor

120
00:04:33.569 --> 00:04:36.779
used with the Raycast nav mesh.

121
00:04:36.779 --> 00:04:39.740
These testing actors can be configured with a query filter,

122
00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:42.220
another actor to test the navigation between,

123
00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:46.060
and optional tests to be run when attempting to navigate.

124
00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:47.740
The testing actor can also show

125
00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:50.019
other debug information related to pathfinding,

126
00:04:50.019 --> 00:04:52.740
such as its path lane, its linked lanes,

127
00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.420
smoothing, and the BV tree query.

128
00:04:56.420 --> 00:05:00.420
Finally, we have the zone graph annotation testing actor.

129
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:03.040
This actor allows for any number of tests to be run

130
00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:06.180
to simulate annotations at a specific point.

131
00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:07.639
Custom tests can also be created

132
00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:10.910
to match a project's specific needs.

133
00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:13.589
Let's take a look at how we create a lane profile.

134
00:05:13.589 --> 00:05:15.709
To create a lane profile, you simply do the following.

135
00:05:15.709 --> 00:05:17.850
Go to project settings, zone graph,

136
00:05:17.850 --> 00:05:21.819
and press the plus button to create a new lane profile.

137
00:05:21.819 --> 00:05:22.980
In the example we see above,

138
00:05:22.980 --> 00:05:26.550
we define a new profile that we've named two-way traffic.

139
00:05:26.550 --> 00:05:28.269
We then add two lanes to this profile,

140
00:05:28.269 --> 00:05:30.129
both with a width of 150,

141
00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:32.250
and we define the direction of both of these lanes,

142
00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:35.750
one being forwards and one being backwards.

143
00:05:35.750 --> 00:05:39.610
And that concludes Introduction to AI in Unreal Engine 5.

144
00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:41.449
Today we learned about AI controllers

145
00:05:41.449 --> 00:05:42.370
and how to control them,

146
00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:44.689
either through behavior trees or state trees,

147
00:05:44.689 --> 00:05:46.170
as well as how to store information

148
00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:49.120
for use in them on the blackboard.

149
00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:50.579
We've looked at the navigation mesh

150
00:05:50.579 --> 00:05:54.519
and how we can query information about the world with EQS.

151
00:05:54.519 --> 00:05:56.459
We've learned what forms of Perception AI

152
00:05:56.459 --> 00:05:58.459
can use straight out of the box.

153
00:05:58.459 --> 00:06:00.740
We've also taken a look at some of the brand new features

154
00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:03.259
in Unreal's toolset, the smart objects,

155
00:06:03.259 --> 00:06:06.459
mass entity, and zone graph systems.

156
00:06:06.459 --> 00:06:09.360
Thank you so much for watching, and have a fantastic day.
